⟦P0⟧Tutorial: Get started with Go⟦P1⟧In this tutorial, you'll get a brief introduction to Go programming. Along the⟦P2⟧way, you will:⟦P3⟧Install Go (if you haven't already).⟦P4⟧Write some simple "Hello, world" code.⟦P5⟧Use the ⟦P6⟧ command to run your code.⟦P7⟧Use the Go package discovery tool to find packages you can use in your own⟦P8⟧code.⟦P9⟧Call functions of an external module.⟦P10⟧⟦P11⟧Note:⟦P12⟧ For other tutorials, see⟦P13⟧⟦P14⟧Tutorials⟦P15⟧.⟦P16⟧Prerequisites⟦P17⟧⟦P18⟧Some programming experience.⟦P19⟧ The code here is pretty⟦P20⟧simple, but it helps to know something about functions.⟦P21⟧⟦P22⟧A tool to edit your code.⟦P23⟧ Any text editor you have will⟦P24⟧work fine. Most text editors have good support for Go. The most popular are⟦P25⟧VSCode (free), ⟦P26⟧ (paid), and Vim (free).⟦P27⟧⟦P28⟧A command terminal.⟦P29⟧ Go works well using any terminal on⟦P30⟧Linux and Mac, and on ⟦P31⟧ or cmd in Windows.⟦P32⟧Install Go⟦P33⟧Just use the ⟦P34⟧Download and install⟦P35⟧ steps.⟦P36⟧Write some code⟦P37⟧Get started with Hello, World.⟦P38⟧Open a command prompt and cd to your home directory.⟦P39⟧On Linux or Mac:⟦P40⟧On Windows:⟦P41⟧Create a hello directory for your first Go source code.⟦P42⟧For example, use the following commands:⟦P43⟧Enable dependency tracking for your code.⟦P44⟧When your code imports packages contained in other modules, you manage⟦P45⟧those dependencies through your code's own module. That module is defined⟦P46⟧by a ⟦P47⟧ file that tracks the modules that provide those packages. That⟦P48⟧⟦P49⟧ file stays with your code, including in your source code⟦P50⟧repository.⟦P51⟧To enable dependency tracking for your code by creating a ⟦P52⟧ file, run⟦P53⟧the⟦P54⟧⟦P55⟧⟦P56⟧⟦P57⟧ command,⟦P58⟧giving it the name of the module your code will be in. The name is the⟦P59⟧module's module path.⟦P60⟧In actual development, the module path will typically be the repository⟦P61⟧location where your source code will be kept. For example, the module⟦P62⟧path might be ⟦P63⟧. If you plan to publish⟦P64⟧your module for others to use, the module path ⟦P65⟧must⟦P66⟧ be a⟦P67⟧location from which Go tools can download your module. For more about⟦P68⟧naming a module with a module path, see⟦P69⟧⟦P70⟧Managing⟦P71⟧dependencies⟦P72⟧.⟦P73⟧For the purposes of this tutorial, just use⟦P74⟧⟦P75⟧.⟦P76⟧In your text editor, create a file ⟦P77⟧ in which to write your code.⟦P78⟧Paste the following code into your ⟦P79⟧ file and save the file.⟦P80⟧This is your Go code. In this code, you:⟦P81⟧Declare a ⟦P82⟧ package (a package is a way to group⟦P83⟧functions, and it's made up of all the files in the same directory).⟦P84⟧Import the popular⟦P85⟧⟦P86⟧⟦P87⟧ package⟦P88⟧,⟦P89⟧which contains functions for formatting text, including printing to the⟦P90⟧console. This package is one of the⟦P91⟧⟦P92⟧standard library⟦P93⟧ packages you got⟦P94⟧when you installed Go.⟦P95⟧Implement a ⟦P96⟧ function to print a message to the⟦P97⟧console. A ⟦P98⟧ function executes by default when you run⟦P99⟧the ⟦P100⟧ package.⟦P101⟧Run your code to see the greeting.⟦P102⟧The⟦P103⟧⟦P104⟧⟦P105⟧⟦P106⟧ command⟦P107⟧is one of many ⟦P108⟧ commands you'll use to get things done with⟦P109⟧Go. Use the following command to get a list of the others:⟦P110⟧Call code in an external package⟦P111⟧When you need your code to do something that might have been implemented by⟦P112⟧someone else, you can look for a package that has functions you can use in⟦P113⟧your code.⟦P114⟧Make your printed message a little more interesting with a function from an⟦P115⟧external module.⟦P116⟧Visit ⟦P117⟧ and⟦P118⟧⟦P119⟧search for a "quote" package⟦P120⟧.⟦P121⟧In the search results, locate and click on the v1 of the ⟦P122⟧ package⟦P123⟧(it should be listed with the "Other major versions" of ⟦P124⟧).⟦P125⟧In the ⟦P126⟧Documentation⟦P127⟧ section, under ⟦P128⟧Index⟦P129⟧, note the⟦P130⟧list of functions you can call from your code. You'll use the⟦P131⟧⟦P132⟧ function.⟦P133⟧At the top of this page, note that package ⟦P134⟧ is⟦P135⟧included in the ⟦P136⟧ module.⟦P137⟧You can use the ⟦P138⟧ site to find published modules whose packages⟦P139⟧have functions you can use in your own code. Packages are published in⟦P140⟧modules ⟦P141⟧ like ⟦P142⟧ ⟦P143⟧ where others can use them.⟦P144⟧Modules are improved with new versions over time, and you can upgrade your⟦P145⟧code to use the improved versions.⟦P146⟧In your Go code, import the ⟦P147⟧ package and add a call⟦P148⟧to its ⟦P149⟧ function.⟦P150⟧After adding the highlighted lines, your code should include the⟦P151⟧following:⟦P152⟧Add new module requirements and sums.⟦P153⟧Go will add the ⟦P154⟧ module as a requirement, as well as a⟦P155⟧⟦P156⟧ file for use in authenticating the module. For more, see⟦P157⟧⟦P158⟧Authenticating modules⟦P159⟧ in the Go⟦P160⟧Modules Reference.⟦P161⟧Run your code to see the message generated by the function you're calling.⟦P162⟧Notice that your code calls the ⟦P163⟧ function, printing a⟦P164⟧clever message about communication.⟦P165⟧When you ran ⟦P166⟧, it located and downloaded the⟦P167⟧⟦P168⟧ module that contains the package you imported.⟦P169⟧By default, it downloaded the latest version ⟦P170⟧ ⟦P171⟧.⟦P172⟧Write more code⟦P173⟧With this quick introduction, you got Go installed and learned some of the⟦P174⟧basics. To write some more code with another tutorial, take a look at⟦P175⟧⟦P176⟧Create a Go module⟦P177⟧.⟦P178⟧
